{"context":"eka-steward 2.0.0 ships the orchestrator core (control loop, planner, pool, guard, approval, secrets, single EKA write path, observability) but exposes no consumable surface: the binary has only `version`, the engine lives in `internal/*` (not importable by any other module), and the human CLI is not a machine interface. The intended consumer is a desktop application (Atrium) whose stack is undecided and may be non-Go (Qt Quick/QML) or polyglot. A Go library would therefore serve only Go consumers; a human CLI cannot be driven programmatically at all; MCP was already rejected as the driver because a request/response tool host is the wrong shape for a long-running supervising loop. The consumer boundary must be language-agnostic, stable and versioned.","decision":"eka-steward exposes a versioned, language-agnostic machine protocol as its product surface: JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio by default (optional local unix socket for long-running use), with initialize-time version negotiation, additive-only evolution, idempotency keys, deterministic responses, and a live notification stream for monitoring. The human subcommands (`run`, `version`) are thin adapters over the same engine; `serve` hosts the protocol. The engine stays in `internal/*` and is NOT promoted to a public Go package until a real Go consumer needs in-process embedding. The binary remains standalone (ADR-0001 stands); the protocol is how consumers attach to it.","consequences":"Any language (C++/QML, Rust, TypeScript, Go) can drive steward by spawning the binary and speaking the protocol. The protocol becomes a contract that must be versioned and covered by golden transcripts, and idempotency/cancel/determinism semantics become part of that contract. Two surfaces (human CLI + protocol) must stay coherent over one engine. `internal/guard`, `internal/approval` and `internal/obs` gain first-class consumers outside the process. Cost: a protocol layer and its spec to maintain, plus a golden-transcript test suite. No Go public API surface is added, so the internal refactor freedom is preserved.","alternativesConsidered":"CLI-only: rejected — a human CLI cannot be consumed by a desktop app and forces screen-scraping. Go library embed: rejected for now — only Go consumers benefit, the consumer stack is undecided/non-Go, and promoting `internal/*` creates a large API surface to maintain; revisit only for a concrete Go consumer. MCP server as the driver surface: rejected (ADR-0001) — a request/response tool host is the wrong shape for a long-running supervising loop; MCP remains appropriate for knowledge operations (eka-mcp), not for orchestration. gRPC: rejected — protobuf toolchain and codegen cost for a local, single-consumer process; JSON-RPC over stdio/socket is dependency-free and trivially consumable."}